# Recycling, resources & AI disclosures

## adi-sms

I left my default suite of IDE (CLion via local Gateway) static analysis LSP tooling throughout the completion of
this project for all work, i.e. how my editor environment is generally set up. Even before the LLMs came rolling
through, LSP tooling in the IntelliJ IDEs has been quite good, and I know my chops at arbitrary syntax retrieval without
such tools have suffered for it.

A local, bespoke model based on GLM 5.3 Flash and a bespoke tool harness was used liberally for formatting and
establishing a watertight Dhall spec, as well as throughout the QuickCheck PBT and Monoid composition in Haskell.
Same with the sealing up of my nix flake file.
Hey, I contribute to llama.cpp too!

Similar to above, while general monkeying around docs and examples without an agent intermediary is still my status
quo, I called my (locally hosted) search engine, Wikipedia, Python docs, arXiv, Stack Overflow and a variety of other
web resources via a bespoke MCP gateway through the IntelliJ agent throughout the development process, particularly for
gathering reference material.

The v2 rework on 2026-10-11 (the live producer, the shared queue, dynamic senders and the Rust Streams consumer) was
also built with coding agents, working under my rulings, reviews and gates.

## adi.clients.xoxd.ai

The demo repository, slides etc etc etc has been broadly ripped off from the stack I built for a Machine Learning demo
and talk I am delivering at XORuby Montreal; while I am unsure if that repo and its slides are public at the time of
your reading this, I want to make it clear the demo stack, reveal.js work and code was already pretty much in place.

Similarly, the XORuby talk repo *itself* is derived from a personal scaffold template repo, *itself* being a derivation
from a variety of personal bazel packages I've slowly built up over time for demos just like this, which has included
portions of code generated by various AI tools including remote LLMs not limited to Zhipu GLM, Moonshot Kimi, OpenAI
via Codex and Anthropic via Claude Code.

Many of the core upstream packages I rely on including Skeleton UI and Effect packages make increasing use of AI
generated code, particularly the code in Effect v4.

## adi-sms-ports

I used Claude Code to assist in the completion of the multithreaded implementation of my fold algorithm in Rust.
